AIM-AI Across Three Collections: A First-Glance Synthesis of Instructor AI Examples from Georgia Tech, Stanford, and Toronto (Version 2)
Oct 5, 2026 · @David Gibson
Purpose and Scope
We have screened three institutional collections of instructor-authored AI examples, totalling 147 entries, and all three appear workable as corpora for the Interactional Model of AI-Mediated Learning (AIM-AI). Before we commit to deep reading and classification, several design decisions must still be settled. This synthesis brings our first-glance screens together so that a colleague can see the evidence behind those decisions and weigh in on them.
The three collections are the Georgia Tech Academic AI Playbook (Joyner & Roy, 2026), Stanford's AIMES Library of Examples (Stanford Center for Teaching and Learning, n.d.), and the University of Toronto's teaching examples, curated by its Centre for Teaching Support & Innovation (Centre for Teaching Support & Innovation [CTSI], n.d.). Each was screened with the same question in mind: does an entry describe a human-AI interaction arrangement that the AIM-AI codebook could classify?
Version 2 revises the synthesis against Codebook v0.5, which realigns our coding instrument to the 14 September 2026 revision of the AIM-AI manuscript. That revision reduces the framework from seven dimensions to five, adopts the mediation episode as its unit of analysis, recasts the configurations as reference points on a continuum, and adds hidden epistemology and AI literacy as explicit constructs. The screening counts are unchanged, because v0.5 carried every screening decision forward. What changes is how we describe the framework, how each collection maps onto the new unit hierarchy, and which open questions remain. The Addendum lists every change from Version 1.
Every count reported here is provisional. The Georgia Tech screen rested on the opening of each article's Action section plus targeted full-text checks. The Stanford and Toronto screens rested mainly on listing pages, titles, and curator categories, with close reads of two and four entries respectively. None of the counts should be cited as findings; they are working estimates that show us where the boundary problems lie.
The AIM-AI Framework and Codebook v0.5
The revised AIM-AI describes interactional mediation as a continuum along which the distribution of participation, cognitive responsibility, interpretive influence, and task direction shifts between AI and human (AIM-AI manuscript, 2026). Four configurations serve as reference points on that continuum, with deliberately permeable boundaries, and each is characterized across five dimensions (Table 1). The continuum is descriptive, not hierarchical: the value of any configuration depends on the goals and context of the learning activity.
