Three features of the revision matter most for coding instructor narratives. First, interaction structure now absorbs the former human-role and AI-role columns, so who steers and who produces the substance are read together. Second, interpretive influence is specified through five interpretive functions: framing the problem, identifying relevant information, proposing explanations, evaluating alternatives, and determining meaning. Third, the revision grounds hidden epistemology in research on bias in computational tools and language models (Caliskan et al., 2017; Litts et al., 2021), on cognitive imperialism (Ofosu-Asare, 2025), and on epistemic authority in algorithmic learning spaces (Jose et al., 2025). It holds that hidden epistemology remains possible in every configuration; what varies is the learner's opportunity to recognize, evaluate, challenge, or redirect the assumptions AI introduces.
The Unit of Analysis
The revision adopts the mediation episode as its primary unit: a coherent sequence of human-AI interactions organized around one learning or task objective. Episodes can shift along the continuum while their objective stays constant, and successive episodes within one learning activity can sit at different points. Codebook v0.5 therefore uses three nested units (rule R1, adopted): the document, as the collection presents it; the learning activity, a distinct human-AI arrangement within the document; and the mediation episode, which is the coding row. Where a narrative describes an arrangement only at the level of design, we code one episode for that activity and memo that episodes were not resolvable.
The Codebook
Codebook v0.5 operationalizes 20 intersections (four configurations by five dimensions), each with operational definitions, inclusion indicators, boundary rules, and cue words. Dimensions are coded independently, so a co-mediated structure can sit alongside authority positioning, and that misalignment is recorded rather than corrected. Coherence across dimensions is summarized with an adjacency-weighted index that treats a split between neighbouring configurations as less incoherent than a split between distant ones; the ordering rests on distributed responsibility and student agency, not on engagement depth, and so departs deliberately from ICAP's own hierarchy (Chi & Wylie, 2014).
The codebook also records cross-cutting extension codes on every episode (Table 2). These capture constructs the revision introduces, and several bear directly on the boundary problems this synthesis identifies.
