Georgia Tech Academic AI Playbook
Of the playbook's 67 articles, 55 are provisionally codable under AIM-AI, and almost half of those describe more than one learning activity.
What the Collection Is
The playbook began in January 2026 with a campus-wide call that drew 139 submissions from all seven colleges and several institutes and support units (Joyner & Roy, 2026). The editors invited 73 initiatives to submit full articles, received 38 further proposals in a second call, and produced 67 articles. Each article is framed as a "play," a contextualized initiative reported under four fixed headings: Formation, Action, Result, and Lessons. The volume closes with 20 lessons synthesized by the editors.
For our purposes, the article is an unusually good document unit. It is written by the instructors who ran the initiative, it reports what happened rather than what was planned, and its Result and Lessons sections supply indirect evidence of how learners actually oriented toward AI.
How the Collection Organizes Itself
The three top-level categories come from the original call: Using AI (20 articles), Teaching AI (35), and Reacting to AI (12). Each article declares its own category and one of ten sub-categories, and all 67 match their table-of-contents placement.
The sub-categories are only rough proxies for interaction arrangement. "AI for Course Preparation & Production" reliably signals instructor-AI arrangements. "Pedagogical Redesign in Response to AI," by contrast, holds both removal designs and designs that actively integrate AI. Teaching AI also contains several articles in which students simply use AI for disciplinary work, such as auditing accounting standards or generating design inputs. The categories record how authors framed their contribution, not how participation was organized.
