Two features of the codable set matter for later analysis. First, instructor activities inflate the multiple-activity count. Six articles (1, 13, 15, 18, 37, 50) have multiple activities largely because they add an instructor-AI arrangement to a student-facing one, and four single-activity articles (3, 4, 5, 10) are instructor-only. Second, some articles split within a single tool. SMART Tutor (8) offers three modes, and the GAUF framework (58) defines five use levels.
Fit to the v0.5 Unit Hierarchy
The playbook maps onto the three nested units without strain: article, then learning activity, then episode. Episode resolution will vary. Action sections often narrate sequence, so many activities should yield more than one episode, or a single episode with a coded trajectory. The within-tool splits now have a natural home: SMART Tutor's modes and GAUF's use levels can be coded as episodes with different objectives inside one activity, rather than forcing a choice between one segment and several.
We expect the mediational literacy codes to matter most here, in the 35 Teaching AI articles, many of which aim to build students' awareness of how AI shapes their thinking. The Result sections should also make the epistemic opportunity codes workable, because instructors often report whether students accepted, noticed, or contested what the AI produced.
What Georgia Tech Taught Us About Screening
The playbook generated three of our screening rules. The non-interactional category (R14) arose because ten articles treat AI as subject matter (algorithmic fairness, analytics models, unplugged curricula) or describe policy and resource development, leaving nothing for the codebook to code. The mixed-article provision arose because many Reacting to AI plays remove AI from one part of a course while describing AI-mediated activity elsewhere. The decision to code instructor-AI arrangements separately arose because the Using AI category is dominated by instructors' own use.
Stanford AIMES Library of Examples
Of Stanford's 40 entries, 19 are provisionally codable, but each holds thin evidence and describes arrangements as designed rather than enacted.
What the Collection Is
The AIMES Library is curated by Stanford's Center for Teaching and Learning (Stanford Center for Teaching and Learning, n.d.). Every example comes from a Stanford faculty member, lecturer, or instructor and has been used in an actual class. The artifacts include course policies, assignments, learning goals, in-class activities, and teaching strategies. The library takes no institutional position on AI.
At the time of our survey the library held 40 entries from 24 courses and one department-level guidance document. Several courses contribute more than one artifact: Technology, Innovation, and Great Power Competition and Human-Centered AI contribute four each, and Media, Mind, and Black Mirror contributes three.
How the Collection Organizes Itself
The main organizing scheme is a stance filter. "Assigned" means students are told to use AI for particular tasks, "Limited" means instructors restrict AI to particular tasks or domains, and "Prohibited" means no AI use is allowed. Our count is 18 Assigned, 15 Limited, and 7 Prohibited. Further filters cover resource type and discipline.
The stance filter classifies permission, not participation. One course can carry different stance labels on different artifacts: Media, Mind, and Black Mirror pairs a Limited policy with Assigned assignments. "Limited" policies can also specify quite definite arrangements. The Design for Behavior Change policy bars AI from reflection, ethics discussions, and sketchnotes, permits it for brainstorming and research support at project milestones, and asks students to submit prompts and transcripts and reflect on where the tool fell short. That is a codable arrangement despite a label that suggests restriction.
Genre
Stanford entries are not narratives. Each has a course-information block, a short "Pedagogy" framing paragraph that appears to be written by Center staff, and the artifact itself in the instructor's words. The Media, Mind, and Black Mirror final project shows both the strength and the limit of this genre. Students customize a personal AI chat partner, talk with it two or three times a week, complete weekly reflective surveys, and write an auto-observational study. The arrangement is precisely specified, but the entry does not report how it played out.
