Three patterns stand out. Learning-goals entries behave like non-interactional commentaries even when labelled "Assigned," because they state outcomes about AI rather than describing an arrangement. Most borderline cases sit where AI is both the object of study and something students handle, such as evaluating models in a legislative simulation or prototyping an AI music application. That boundary is denser here than at Georgia Tech, because many of these courses are about AI. Finally, the seven Prohibited entries are only provisionally excluded; some may describe a permitted arrangement once read in full.
Fit to the v0.5 Unit Hierarchy
Stanford fits the hierarchy least cleanly. If the course is the document, each artifact becomes a learning activity, which gives about 25 course profiles and mirrors the playbook roll-up. If the artifact is the document, we have 40 thin records with one activity each. Either way, episodes will rarely be resolvable, so the R1 fallback of one episode per activity will be the norm.
Two v0.5 features help here. Under R3, nearly every Stanford episode will carry the evidence locus "design intent," so its holistic configuration describes the intended arrangement, and the design-enactment comparison simply does not compute. That makes the genre limit explicit in the data rather than hiding it. Second, course policies that enumerate permitted uses match an existing indicator: the HD-2 code lists restricted or enumerated permitted uses as evidence of a human-directed structure. Policies that ask students to reflect on where a tool fell short, as Design for Behavior Change does, also design for the epistemic opportunity and mediational literacy codes, although only as intent.
University of Toronto Teaching Examples
Of Toronto's 40 entries, 20 are provisionally codable, and they include some of the most precisely specified arrangements in any of the three collections.
What the Collection Is
The collection is curated by U of T's Centre for Teaching Support & Innovation and presents examples of how instructors have approached generative AI: limiting its use, communicating expectations, integrating it into coursework, or building a course-specific chatbot with Cogniti (CTSI, n.d.). It is open and growing; CTSI invites instructors to submit assessments, learning activities, or communication strategies through an online form.
At the time of our survey the page listed 40 entries from 27 instructors: 12 Limiting, 6 Communicating, 21 Integrating, and 1 Cogniti. Instructors recur across entries. Thavarajah and Kyle Smith contribute four each, spanning policy, transparency modeling, oral assessment, and integration designs, and seven others contribute two.
How the Collection Organizes Itself
Toronto's scheme mixes two classification axes. Three categories describe an instructional stance, while the fourth names a platform. Cogniti is a generative AI chatbot tailored to course materials, and CTSI notes that the section is still being updated as use cases develop. A platform category says nothing about stance, and a stance category says nothing about tooling.
Stance labels also do not travel across collections. Stanford's "Limited" meant AI restricted to particular tasks, often within codable policies. Toronto's "Limiting AI Use" mostly means assessment redesigned to resist AI, through oral exams, handwritten journals, and in-class work, which is closer to Georgia Tech's removal plays. Even so, the category is not uniform. Thavarajah's lab redesign moves graded post-lab work into the lab session, while its open-book pre-lab quiz explicitly allows students to use AI tools to support their learning: an R7 mixed case.
Genre
Toronto entries are a hybrid. Most follow a CTSI template: instructor biography, Objectives, and a step-by-step Process far more granular than Stanford's artifacts. Wong's reading response assignment illustrates the granularity. Students write a reaction to the readings, prompt ChatGPT for a reply of 50 words or fewer, respond with further analysis grounded in the readings, and repeat, so that they interrogate rather than passively accept AI output. Few entries in any collection specify interaction structure and interpretive influence so clearly.
Older entries close with a "Future-Focused Skill Development" section mapping the activity onto the University of Calgary's STRIVE model (Taylor Institute for Teaching and Learning, n.d.), which reads as curator framing. Newer entries add outcomes in the instructor's voice, moving toward Georgia Tech's reflective genre. Kaur's Cogniti entry reports, for example, that 75% of students agreed or strongly agreed that the project helped them understand how system prompts shape AI behaviour.
