Four patterns stand out. First, Toronto's categories predict our screen better than either other scheme: nearly all Limiting entries are exclusion candidates, and nearly all Integrating entries look codable. The variation sits inside the codable set, which is where AIM-AI should do its work.
Second, co-created policies are not interactions, even though they produce rules about use. Moghaddam's class reached consensus to permit AI for revising outlines and essays and for brainstorming, but not for generating ideas, arguments, or claims. These are permissions with no human-AI arrangement in the activity itself.
Third, the "Evaluating & Critiquing AI Output" sub-heading raises a boundary we had not met. In some designs the instructor may generate the AI output and students critique it without ever touching the system.
Fourth, the single Cogniti entry is among the richest in any collection. Students designed their own agents in Cogniti's Sandbox, customizing the interface, opening message, and system prompt, and then peer-reviewed other groups' personas for whether they stayed in character and avoided fabricating data. Students as AI designers is a role our codebook does not yet name.
Fit to the v0.5 Unit Hierarchy
Toronto offers the best episode-level resolution of the three collections, because its Process sections are written as numbered steps with distinct objectives. The entry is a natural document, and most entries hold one learning activity. Kaur's project, for instance, separates into at least two episodes with different objectives: building a grounded persona, then reviewing other groups' personas. Wong's dialogue is better read as one episode with a coded trajectory, since its objective stays constant across the three turns. Thavarajah's lab report shows how R1 and R7 work together: the pre-lab quiz is a codable episode, while the in-lab work, from which AI is removed, is recorded but not coded.
The extension codes also find ready evidence here. Wong's design builds in an epistemic move at the Challenged level, and the "Evaluating & Critiquing AI Output" entries are designed around the Recognized and Evaluated levels. Kaur's outcome data test a boundary in the mediational literacy rule: understanding how system prompts shape AI behaviour could count as framing awareness, or as the prompting knowledge that R17 excludes.
