Three Findings From the Comparison
The genres form a continuum of evidence locus. Georgia Tech narratives report enacted arrangements with outcomes, Stanford artifacts specify designed arrangements without outcomes, and Toronto entries sit between them. Codebook v0.5 now records this directly: every episode carries an evidence locus, an intended configuration, and, where enactment evidence exists, a computed design-enactment alignment (R3). The revised manuscript's position that design establishes conditions but does not determine engagement makes the distinction substantive, not merely methodological. Positioning, interpretive influence, and epistemic opportunity describe what learners actually did with AI; a design can only state what it hoped for. Any cross-collection comparison of configurations will therefore be confounded with genre unless evidence locus is held constant or modelled.
Curator labels classify something other than interaction, and each in a different way. Georgia Tech classifies by the author's framing of the contribution (using, teaching, or reacting), Stanford by permission (assigned, limited, prohibited), and Toronto by a mix of stance and platform. None classifies how participation is organized. The same word also means different things across schemes: Stanford's "Limited" often marks a codable arrangement, whereas Toronto's "Limiting" usually marks an AI-resistant redesign. This inconsistency is itself evidence for the AIM-AI argument that "AI use" labels obscure interactional variation, a point the revised manuscript now supports with a systematic review finding that studies describe "AI use," "exposure," or "assistance" without characterizing the interaction (Liang et al., 2026).
The boundary problems recur, but at different densities. Every collection has AI-as-subject entries, mixed removal designs, and instructor-AI arrangements. Stanford adds a dense AI-as-subject boundary because many of its courses are about AI. Toronto adds two boundaries that v0.5 does not yet resolve: relayed AI output, where students critique output they never generated, and students as designers of AI agents.
