What the First Glance Suggests About AIM-AI's Analytic Utility
Codebook v0.5 strengthens the case for AIM-AI as an analytic lens for these collections. It now names the design-enactment gap that Version 1 treated as a strain, and it adds codes for the epistemic work that many of these designs are built around. Its most distinctive constructs, however, remain the hardest to evidence from instructor-authored documents.
Where the Framework Already Adds Value
AIM-AI separates documents that the curators' labels group together. Two Toronto entries filed under "AI as a Research & Writing Tool" illustrate the point. Wong's dialogues build in a turn-by-turn structure in which students must answer the AI with evidence from the readings, which suggests a co-mediated configuration on interaction structure and interpretive influence. A literature review assignment in which students collect and summarize AI-retrieved sources could, depending on its details, sit nearer the AI-structured reference point. The same curator label covers two different distributions of interpretive responsibility, and only the framework names the difference.
The epistemic opportunity codes add a second axis of discrimination that cuts across configuration. Toronto's entire "Evaluating & Critiquing AI Output" sub-heading, and Stanford policies that ask students to report where a tool fell short, are designed to move learners beyond inheriting AI-introduced assumptions. Under v0.5 we can record that design move separately from the configuration, which lets us examine the revised manuscript's claim that hidden epistemology persists in every configuration.
The framework also explains some of what the collections exclude. Many "Limiting" and "Reacting" designs can be read as instructors redistributing cognitive responsibility toward the human, sometimes by removing AI entirely. That reading connects the excluded R7 designs back to the framework's central concern, even though we do not code them.
Where the Framework Will Be Strained
Version 1 identified three strains. Codebook v0.5 resolves one, narrows another, and leaves the third open, and it adds a fourth.
• Positioning and interpretation from designs (largely resolved). R3 now records evidence locus and intended configuration for every episode, so designed and enacted positioning are no longer conflated. What remains is an analytic consequence: for Stanford, and for older Toronto entries, almost every configuration we code will be an intended configuration.
• Roles the framework does not name (narrowed). The merged interaction structure dimension reads who steers and who produces the substance together, which absorbs instructors modeling their own AI use as an ordinary instructor episode (R2). Students designing AI agents (Kaur) and students critiquing output generated by someone else (Spot the Error) still have no clear home. The second is not covered by R8, which addresses AI that mediates relations among humans.
• AI as subject versus AI as participant (open). We kept R14 unchanged under v0.5, but the Stanford borderline set suggests we still need a sharper criterion, perhaps whether learners engage the system as a source of contributions or only inspect it as an artifact.
• Mediational literacy versus technical AI knowledge (new). R17 excludes knowledge of AI capabilities and prompting technique from the literacy codes. Many Teaching AI articles and AI-themed courses sit close to that line, and Kaur's finding that students learned how system prompts shape AI behaviour is a clean test case.
A Note on Comparability
The three collections are not samples of instructor practice in any statistical sense. Each is curated, and each curator's call shaped what was submitted. Georgia Tech's call favoured mature, reportable initiatives; Stanford's library spans a deliberate range of stances; Toronto's open call is still accumulating. Cross-collection comparisons of configuration frequencies should therefore be treated as descriptions of what each institution chose to showcase, not as estimates of how AI is used at each institution.
